		(property "Description" "Panasonic AXK580137YG -- P5K series 0.5mm-pitch 80-pin board-to-board SOCKET, module side (confirmed on the ALINX AC7200 module; mates with the carrier board's own AXK680337YG header -- see ../../AC7200/AC7200_README.md). Real dimensions from Panasonic/Mouser's own P5K/P5KS catalog PDF (mouser.com/catalog/specsheets/AXK.pdf), read directly: body 23.20 x 19.50mm (A x B, same as the header), 0.5mm pitch, 40 pins/row x 2 rows staggered by 0.25mm (half pitch), pad 0.25 x 0.75mm, total footprint span 6.80mm (wider than the header's 5.60mm -- read directly off the Socket's own land-pattern drawing, not assumed equal to the header). Part number CONFIRMED, not assumed: the AC7200 module's own STEP model (AC7200.3.0.stp) names this connector 'AXK680137YG', which doesn't decode against Panasonic's real P5K part-number table (leading digit 6 = Header selector, but mated-height digit 1 is documented Socket-only) -- flagged as a likely STEP-model typo when this part was first built. ALINX's own real schematic (docs/AC7200_Schematic.pdf, page 12 'AC7200 CONNECTOR') settles it: all 4 connector reference labels explicitly read 'AXK580137YG', confirming the STEP model's name was wrong and this (leading digit 5) is the real part. All 320 CON1-4 signal-pin assignments in ../../AC7200/KiCad/AC7200.kicad_sym were also cross-checked pin-by-pin against that same real schematic -- exact match, no discrepancies. Pin numbering on THIS standalone connector symbol (1-80 sequential by row) is still an ASSUMED convention, matching the header's own same assumption -- the real schematic only labels FPGA bank/ball nets on CON1-4, not this connector's own physical pin-to-number layout, so it wasn't independently verifiable against a real pin-1 diagram in the catalog either."
			(at 0 0 0)
			(show_name no) (do_not_autoplace no) (hide yes)
